---
/**
 * /data-handling — How we handle your data.
 *
 * Built Aug 26 2026 from docs/legal/data-handling.md, which Fredrick supplied the same
 * day.
 *
 * HELD ON A BRANCH, alongside /privacy. NOT FOR MERGE UNTIL REVIEWED. The copy file's
 * own header says "Draft for review. Do not publish as written". This page is less
 * lawyer-dependent than /privacy, but it is MORE exposed in a different way: it makes
 * factual commitments about how the work is actually done, and a client who was told
 * something here and finds otherwise mid-engagement has a specific, quotable sentence.
 * `noindex` is set as a second line of defence, not as the hold.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE IS FOR, and why it is not a duplicate of /privacy: privacy covers
 * personal information collected through the website. This page covers the thing a
 * prospective client actually worries about — what happens when ArkSystems is given
 * access to their systems and, in some engagements, to records belonging to their own
 * clients. The /accounting FAQ answers that in three sentences and links here for the
 * rest. THAT DIVISION IS DELIBERATE and holds the /accounting page rule in place: the
 * industry page stays a builder's page with no compliance framing, and the detail lives
 * on a page the cautious reader chooses to open.
 *
 * THE RULE FOR THIS PAGE: every measure stated here must be one that is in place today.
 * Where a practice is not yet real, CUT THE LINE RATHER THAN SOFTENING IT. A hedged
 * commitment on this page is worse than no commitment, because it reads as evasion to
 * exactly the reader who came here to be reassured.
 *
 * TWO DEPARTURES FROM THE APPROVED COPY, both decided by Fredrick on Aug 26 2026:
 *
 * 1. NO LOCATION LINE. The draft closes the Questions block with "ArkSystems is operated
 *    by FoodyGuru Inc., Metro Vancouver, British Columbia, Canada". The location is gone
 *    under the standing instruction of Aug 25 2026; the entity stays. Same call as
 *    /privacy, taken at the same time.
 *
 * 2. THREE FACTS WERE ANSWERED BY DELETION, not by filling, on Fredrick's answers of Aug
 *    26 2026. That is this page's own rule applied to itself — where a practice is not
 *    settled, the line comes out rather than being softened:
 *
 *    - NO PAYMENT PROVIDER ROW. There is no third-party processor in the path. A stronger
 *      position than a filled row, not a gap.
 *    - NO PASSWORD MANAGER ROW, and no sentence claiming credentials are held in one. See
 *      the note at that paragraph for why half of it could not stay.
 *    - NO BACKUP BULLET. There are no separate backups beyond what Google Workspace holds
 *      natively, so the 90-day deletion commitment stands on its own. The copy file paired
 *      the two deliberately because they run in sequence; with nothing to pair, the claim
 *      is stronger — and worth the practitioner checking against Google Workspace's own
 *      trash and admin-recovery behaviour, which is not the same as nothing.
 *
 *    The access-removal window is confirmed at five business days. THIS PAGE NOW CARRIES NO
 *    BLANKS. The one remaining across all three is the Privacy Officer's surname, on
 *    /privacy.
 *
 * WHAT THIS PAGE MUST NEVER CLAIM, from the copy file's own list. Each is a rule, not a
 * preference:
 *
 * - NO CERTIFICATIONS. No SOC 2, no ISO 27001, no compliance standard ArkSystems does
 *   not hold. This is the single highest-risk claim available on a page like this, it is
 *   trivially checkable, and claiming it would destroy exactly the trust the page exists
 *   to earn. Note that "SOC2 compliant infrastructure" is one of the three claims a
 *   design tool invented for /accounting and that was caught before it shipped.
 * - NO GUARANTEE OF SECURITY. "We do not claim that no incident can occur" is deliberate
 *   and stays.
 * - NO INSURANCE CLAIM unless the policy is bound. Professional liability and cyber cover
 *   are both listed as unevaluated, so neither is mentioned. If either is bound later it
 *   is a genuine advantage on this page — but only then.
 * - NO BLANKET NO-TRAINING PROMISE. Removed from the copy on Aug 26 2026 and it must not
 *   come back. Whether a provider trains on inputs depends on that provider's terms and
 *   tier, both of which change without notice, and a commitment ArkSystems cannot enforce
 *   is the worst kind to publish here. What the page commits to instead is disclosure:
 *   which providers, which tier, what their terms say, before connection. If a specific
 *   build genuinely runs on a no-training tier, say so IN THAT ENGAGEMENT'S DOCUMENTS,
 *   where it is a checkable statement about a named provider — never as a site-wide rule.
 * - NO CLIENT NAMES OR EXAMPLES, here as everywhere.
 *
 * THE RETENTION SECTION IS THE STRONGEST THING ON THE PAGE and it came from Fredrick:
 * no client operational data is retained after implementation. Most firms in this space
 * cannot say it, because their product holds the data. It is stated first and in the
 * shortest sentence available, because a practice deciding whether to let an outside firm
 * near their client files is asking precisely this. VERIFY IT HOLDS LITERALLY BEFORE
 * PUBLISH — it is quotable, and it is the one line on the site most likely to be read
 * back to us.
 *
 * The copy file paired the 90-day deletion window with a backup window because the two run
 * in sequence — a file deleted on day 90 can still sit in a backup taken before it, so
 * merging them into one number would understate the outside figure. THAT PAIRING IS GONE
 * BECAUSE THE BACKUPS ARE, not because the two were merged. If separate backups are ever
 * introduced, the second figure comes back as its own line and is never folded into the
 * first.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import LegalDocument from "../components/legal/LegalDocument.astro";
/* The access-removal window is confirmed, so this renders as ordinary text today.
 * It stays routed through Pending rather than inlined: the value is a tracked fact,
 * and if it is ever set back to null the marked blank returns on its own instead of
 * the page quietly losing a number. */
import Pending from "../components/legal/Pending.astro";
import { PENDING, LEGAL_ENTITY, LEGAL_EMAIL } from "../lib/legal";

const description =
  "What ArkSystems gets access to during an engagement, which tools it passes through, what we keep afterwards, and what we don't keep at all.";
---

<BaseLayout title="How we handle your data" description={description} noindex>
  <LegalDocument title="How we handle your data">
    {/* The opening is body copy, not a styled standfirst — see the note on /privacy. */}
    <p>
      Before an engagement starts, you should know exactly what we get access to, what we do
      with it, which tools it passes through, and what happens when the work ends. This page
      sets that out. If your firm has requirements of its own, tell us before we begin
      &mdash; it changes how we build, and that is a normal thing to specify.
    </p>

    <h2 id="principles">The principles</h2>

    <p>Four commitments that hold across every engagement.</p>

    <p>
        <span class="doc-term">It stays in your systems.</span> What we build operates on
        accounts you own. ArkSystems does not run a platform that holds your records, and
        there is nothing of yours to retrieve or migrate if you stop working with us.
    </p>

    <p>
        <span class="doc-term">We take the least access that does the job.</span> Read-only
        where reading is enough. Scoped to the workflow, not the organization. If a step
        needs broader access, we ask for it specifically and say why.
    </p>

    <p>
        <span class="doc-term">Every tool in the path is named before it is connected.</span>
        You see the list &mdash; hosting, models, storage, anything the information passes
        through &mdash; in writing, before anything is switched on. Nothing gets added
        mid-engagement without telling you.
    </p>

    <p>
        <span class="doc-term">You set the boundary between automatic and reviewed.</span>
        Which steps run without a person, and which stop for one, is your decision. We build
        to it and we record what the system did at each step, so the record exists whether or
        not anyone asks for it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-access">What we access, stage by stage</h2>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">The 20-minute call.</span> Nothing. You describe the problem, we
      ask questions. No access, no documents, no credentials.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Discovery and the workshop.</span> Conversations, process
      walkthroughs, and figures about how the work runs &mdash; volumes, timings, who does
      what. Where we need to see a real example, we ask for a redacted one, and we work from
      screen-shares in preference to copies. We do not need your client records to establish
      where the cost sits.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Implementation.</span> Access to the systems the work touches,
      granted through your own administration and using accounts issued to us &mdash; never
      shared passwords, and never credentials sent by email. Access is scoped to what the
      build requires and no more.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Testing.</span> Synthetic or redacted data wherever the test can
      be run with it. Where a test genuinely requires live records, we agree that
      specifically, in advance, and limit it to what the test needs.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Once it&rsquo;s running.</span> The system touches whatever the
      workflow touches &mdash; and that is defined in writing before it&rsquo;s built, not
      discovered afterwards.
    </p>

    <h2 id="access">Access, and how it ends</h2>

    <p>
      Access is issued by you, through your own systems, so you can see it and remove it
      without asking us.
    </p>

    {/* The approved copy has a second sentence here: "Credentials are held in a password
      * manager, never in documents, email or chat." IT IS CUT ON PURPOSE, by Fredrick's
      * instruction of Aug 26 2026 — first not to name a password manager, then to remove
      * the sentence rather than keep half of it.
      *
      * That is this page's own rule applied to itself: every measure stated here must be
      * one that is in place today, and where a practice is not yet settled the line comes
      * out rather than being softened. A page that describes tooling it cannot name reads
      * as evasion to exactly the reader who came here to be reassured.
      *
      * ONE CONSEQUENCE: the page no longer says anything about where credentials are held.
      * A practice running a vendor assessment asks that question directly, and it now gets
      * answered on the call instead. If a password manager is adopted and named later, the
      * sentence and its provider-table row come back together — one without the other is
      * what was wrong here in the first place. */}
    <p>
      Multi-factor authentication is used on every account that reaches client information.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">When an engagement ends, access ends.</span> We ask you to remove
      our accounts and we confirm removal on our side within{" "}
      <Pending value={PENDING.accessRemovalWindow} label="access-removal window" />. If you
      want that earlier, or immediately, say so &mdash; it is your access to withdraw at any
      point and you do not need a reason.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ai-models">AI models specifically</h2>

    <p>This is the part clients ask about most, so it is stated plainly.</p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Which providers.</span> There is no fixed list, because the
      answer is partly yours. Which models a build uses depends on what the work needs and on
      what your firm will accept &mdash; some practices have already approved a provider, some
      rule one out, some require processing to stay in a particular region. We propose what
      fits, you decide, and the providers and tiers are named in your engagement documents
      before anything is connected.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">What each provider does with your inputs.</span> Providers differ,
      and their terms change. Before anything is connected, we tell you which providers your
      build uses, which tier, and what their terms say about how inputs are handled &mdash;
      including whether they are used for model training. You decide with that in front of you
      rather than afterwards.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Retention at the provider.</span> Providers may retain inputs
      briefly for abuse monitoring under their own terms. Those terms are named to you rather
      than summarized by us, so you can read them yourself.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">What we keep out of models.</span> We design so that information a
      model does not need to do the job does not reach it. Where a step can run on a reference
      rather than the underlying record, it runs on the reference.
    </p>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">Where the processing happens.</span> Most major model providers
      process in the United States. If your firm requires processing to stay in Canada, tell us
      before the design is fixed &mdash; it constrains which providers can be used and it is
      far cheaper to know at the start than to rebuild.
    </p>

    <h2 id="where-processed">Where information is processed</h2>

    <p>ArkSystems&rsquo; own operations run on:</p>

    <div class="doc-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Provider</th>
            <th scope="col">Purpose</th>
            <th scope="col">Region</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Workspace</td>
            <td>Documents, forms, and our client and prospect records</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Namecheap (Private Email)</td>
            <td>Our email &mdash; every message sent to or from our address</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Netlify</td>
            <td>Website hosting</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Cal.com</td>
            <td>Call booking</td>
            <td>United States</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">AI providers are not on this list, deliberately.</span> They are
      chosen per engagement rather than fixed in advance, for the reasons above, and every
      provider inside a delivered system is named &mdash; with its tier and its region &mdash;
      in that engagement&rsquo;s documents before anything is connected.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-we-keep">What we keep after the work ends</h2>

    <p>
      <span class="doc-term">
        We do not keep your operational data. Not during the engagement beyond what the work
        requires, and not after it.
      </span>
    </p>

    <p>
      Anything you give us to examine, test with or build against is working material, not a
      record. It is deleted within 90 days of the engagement closing, and nothing of yours
      remains on our systems once the work is done. The system we built runs on your accounts,
      holding your data, where it always was.
    </p>

    <p>What we do keep is the paperwork of the engagement:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <span class="doc-term">
          Contracts, proposals, statements of work, requirements documents, the workshop
          document and invoices
        </span>{" "}
        &mdash; kept for the length of the relationship and for six years after the end of the
        tax year they relate to, because Canadian tax law requires business records to be
        retained for that period
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="doc-term">Credentials and access</span> &mdash; removed at close, as above
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      You can ask us to delete your business data earlier than the schedule above, and we will
      confirm in writing when it is done.
    </p>

    <h2 id="if-something-goes-wrong">If something goes wrong</h2>

    <p>
      If a security incident affects your information, we tell you &mdash; promptly, with what
      we know at the time, rather than after an internal investigation concludes. We work with
      you to establish what was affected, and we support whatever notification your own
      obligations require.
    </p>

    <p>
      We do not claim that no incident can occur. What we commit to is that you hear about it
      from us, early.
    </p>

    <h2 id="your-responsibility">What stays your responsibility</h2>

    <p>
      You remain responsible for your clients&rsquo; information and for your own professional
      and regulatory obligations. We build to the boundaries you set; we do not determine what
      those boundaries should be, and nothing on this page is advice about your obligations.
    </p>

    <p>
      If your professional body, your insurer or a client contract imposes requirements on how
      information is handled or where it is processed, tell us at the start. Requirements are a
      design input. Discovered late, they are a rebuild.
    </p>

    <h2 id="questions">Questions</h2>

    <p>
      If your firm has a security questionnaire, a vendor assessment or a data-processing
      agreement, send it before we start rather than after. We would rather answer it properly
      than discover halfway through that we cannot.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-contact">
      {/* Plain text, not a mailto: link — see the note on /privacy section 11. */}
      <p>{LEGAL_EMAIL}</p>
      <p>ArkSystems is operated by {LEGAL_ENTITY}.</p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Personal information collected through this website is covered separately, on our{" "}
      <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>.
    </p>
  </LegalDocument>
</BaseLayout>
